<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4669"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:Iwillc@tchu.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Catcher<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Catcher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 5:24 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tom, since everyone is talking about it...why not tell them the truth?  The ice trays were in the Otero crime scene photographs, and it was noticed that there were empty cube holes in the otherwise full tray.  Also, if the ice hadn&#39;t melted yet, then the timeline of the crimes could have been determined by that evidence, or at least refined.  The ice tray evidence was becoming of more considerable importance, when after almost two weeks into the investigation, a Sheriff’s deputy &#40;could have been a detective&#41; admitted that he had helped himself to some ice tea from the victim&#39;s refrigerator, and had forgotten to replace the ice trays.  As he was a first responder, the trays were taken out before the crime scene photographer&#39;s photo shoot, and therefore the ice trays were in the pics.  How does that factual kool-aid add to the mix of &#34;WPD cover-up theories&#34;??????  Very interesting...could it have been the same person that &#34;accidentally&#34; stepped in the blood and left the boot print?  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4669-->
<!--Post: 4675--><!--Time: 1106665360-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4675"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 7:02 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My thoughts are, if indeed an officer left the trays out - and it seems plausible, given the time of day the Otero bodies were discovered and that the ice would likely have melted by then - that it was less of a cover-up than just dismissing the scene - since the officer, coming upon the crime and likely not feeling too well - got himself something cold to drink.   <BR> <BR>Even if BTK had turned off all heat - the house would have retained enough to melt the ice - IMHO anyway.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4675-->
